// =============================================================================
// ABSTRACT (Strictly <= 250 words)
// =============================================================================
#heading(level: 1, numbering: none)[Abstract]

Convergent tooth loss in birds and turtles inactivated enamel matrix protein genes (_ENAM_, _AMBN_, _AMTN_), leaving sequence relics whose detection and dating are confounded by annotation blind spots and structural disorder. Auditing 18 sauropsid RefSeq assemblies plus _Gallus gallus_ `GRCg7b` Ensembl Release 116 shows that both RefSeq Release 106 and Ensembl Release 116 omit all nine tooth genes in `GRCg7b`. Synteny-anchored searches across the _NRG1–LPL_ cluster recover three unannotated chrZ relics: whole-proteome reciprocal `BLASTx`-confirmed _ΨENAM_ Exon 8 (`NC_052572.1:55,155,480–55,157,464`, `−`, 1,985-bp union) and _ΨAMBN_ Exon 3 (`55,168,855–55,168,992`, `−`, 138 bp), nucleotide- and synteny-confirmed _ΨENAM_ Exon 6 (`55,160,529–55,160,677`, `−`; 47-bp core with a `CAA→TAA` stop and 1-nt deletion precluding protein reciprocity), plus reciprocal-confirmed kakapo _ΨAMTN_ (`NC_044302.2:51,216,319–51,216,444`, `−`). Conversely, automated RefSeq Gnomon pipelines emit `LOW QUALITY PROTEIN` `PROTEIN_CODING` models by patching genomic frameshifts and/or stop codons across three confirmed chromosome-level pseudogenes (_Chrysemys picta_ _ENAM_, _Chelonia mydas_ _AMBN_, _Struthio camelus_ _AMELX_) and one scaffold-level locus (_Apteryx rowi_ _AMELX_). Equidistant _Anolis_ queries (\~281 Ma; 1,000 permutations) show that intact osteogenic intrinsically disordered proteins (_IBSP_, _SPP1_) lose 25.5–51.5% of excess `tBLASTn` bitscore in birds—overlapping decayed avian _ENAM_/_AMBN_—whereas globular _LPL_ scores 112.6% of crocodylian levels. Splice-aware `miniprot` alignment detects >150-fold coding-lesion enrichment in edentulous enamel loci (16.98 vs. 0.11 lesions/1,000 aa in toothed controls; 0.00 across 18/18 _LPL_ controls, 133/133 `GT-AG` introns). Dual-aligner consensus and IQ-TREE 3 ancestral reconstruction recover 23 shared lesions dating subclades, zero crown Neornithes synapomorphies, and 31.2–37.5% avian _ENAM_ stop incongruence under topology T1 (56.2–68.8% under alternatives), matching coverage-masked neutral simulations.

