// =============================================================================
// 1. INTRODUCTION
// =============================================================================
#heading(level: 1, numbering: none)[1. Introduction]

Mineralized teeth have been lost independently across multiple vertebrate lineages, including the stem lineages of crown birds (Aves, \~116 Ma) and crown turtles (Testudines, \~157–210 Ma) as well as several placental mammal clades @davitbeal2009 @meredith2013 @meredith2014 @brocklehurst2021. Because tooth enamel mineralization requires a specialized subset of secretory calcium-binding phosphoprotein (_SCPP_) genes—chiefly enamelin (_ENAM_), ameloblastin (_AMBN_), amelotin (_AMTN_), and amelogenin (_AMELX_)—together with maturation factors such as odontogenesis-associated phosphoprotein (_ODAPH_, formerly _C4orf26_) and matrix metalloproteinase 20 (_MMP20_), relaxed purifying selection following edentulism leaves decaying molecular fossils (pseudogenes) embedded in conserved genomic neighborhoods @kawasaki2003 @kawasaki2009 @meredith2009 @springer2016 @liang2021 @kawasaki2023 @chun2025.

Foundational paleogenomic studies established the core architecture of sauropsid enamel-gene decay. #pcite(<sire2008>) identified a degraded amelogenin pseudogene (_ΨAMEL_, `EU340348`) on chicken chromosome 1 while inferring complete deletion of other enamel loci in early chicken assemblies. Shortly thereafter, #pcite(<alhashimi2010>) demonstrated that in sauropsids the _ENAM–AMBN–AMTN_ enamel subcluster resides in a translocated syntenic neighborhood flanked by _NRG1_ and _LPL_—distinct from the ancestral _SPARCL1–SIBLING_ cluster retained on mammalian chromosome 4—and reported the first chicken _ENAM_ pseudogene fragment in that region. Subsequent comparative analyses documented widespread inactivating mutations in _ENAM_, _AMBN_, _AMTN_, _AMELX_, _MMP20_, and _DSPP_ across turtles @meredith2013 and 48 avian genomes @meredith2014, inferring a single loss of mineralized teeth in the common ancestor of crown birds and documenting parallel regression across toothless mammals @springer2019 @mu2021 @randall2022 @emerling2023 @randall2024 @emerling2026. Recent developmental work has also linked ciliary and signaling regulators such as _C2CD3_ to avian tooth suppression alongside structural enamel-gene decay @kantaputra2026.

Despite this empirical foundation, three pervasive methodological vulnerabilities affect modern paleogenomic surveys of tooth-gene loss—and comparative analyses of trait loss more broadly. First, automated gene-annotation pipelines (NCBI RefSeq and Ensembl) exhibit bidirectional errors on degenerating loci: they routinely omit genuine pseudogene fragments even in modern chromosome-level reference genomes while occasionally projecting `PROTEIN_CODING` models onto frame-disrupted pseudogenes by synthetically modifying inferred CDS sequences @weisman2022 @kirilenko2023. Second, enamel matrix proteins are proline- and glutamine-rich intrinsically disordered proteins (IDPs) lacking globular catalytic domains @kawasaki2006 @kawasaki2021 @chun2025. In translated sequence searches (`tBLASTn`), compositional bias, low-complexity filtering (`SEG`), protein length, and clade-specific substitution-rate heterogeneity systematically depress alignment bitscores even for intact, functional IDPs @wootton1993 @wootton1996 @schaffer2001 @yu2003 @yu2005 @altschul2005 @gertz2006 @weisman2020 @chow2024. Treating depressed translated-alignment bitscores as proxies for pseudogenization risks conflating structural disorder with gene inactivation. Third, dating ancient trait losses via shared premature termination codons (PTCs) is vulnerable to mutational convergence (homoplasy), secondary stop-to-sense reversal, and alignment-dependent gap placement @meredith2014 @emerling2026.

To separate empirical replication from original methodological and genomic findings, we designed a deterministic, four-level audit ("Evidence Ladder") across 18 high-contiguity sauropsid RefSeq reference genomes (6 toothed controls and 12 edentulous birds and turtles, anchored against human `GRCh38.p14`; unambiguous six-letter species identifiers in Table S0). While replicating and extending prior paleogenomic discoveries of avian and testudine enamel pseudogenes @alhashimi2010 @meredith2013 @meredith2014, this study makes four original contributions:
1. A dual-database annotation audit (NCBI RefSeq Release 106 and Ensembl Release 116) proving that _all_ nine odontogenic genes remain completely unannotated in the modern chromosome-level reference _Gallus gallus_ `GRCg7b`, accompanied by exact, reconciled 1-based inclusive and 0-based BED coordinates for three unannotated chrZ relics (two whole-proteome reciprocal `BLASTx`-confirmed, one nucleotide- and synteny-confirmed), a reciprocal-confirmed _ΨAMTN_ relic on kakapo (_Strigops habroptila_) chrZ, and a forensic native-CDS audit of four Gnomon `LOW QUALITY PROTEIN` `PROTEIN_CODING` models across edentulous assemblies.
2. A quantitative 18-species homology-bias benchmark calibrated with 1,000 composition-preserving permutations and 200 local 20-aa block-preserving shuffles per query (plus 2,000-replicate bootstrap confidence intervals), contrasting functional osteogenic IDPs (_IBSP_, _SPP1_, _SPARCL1_) against globular flanking _LPL_ under equidistant _Anolis_ queries (\~281 Ma).
3. A splice-aware `miniprot` coding-lesion and `GT-AG` splice-site audit across all 18 _NRG1–LPL_ intervals showing that replacing a truncated legacy GenBank enamelin sequence (`QOX06214.1`, 849 aa) with full-length RefSeq _ENAM_ (`XP_019346377.1`, 1,078 aa) unlocks previously invisible pseudogene exons and canonical `GT→AT` splice-donor disruptions while exposing a 58.4-kb distal low-complexity chaining artifact in _Chrysemys picta_.
4. A dual-aligner (`MAFFT L-INS-i` + `MUSCLE 5`) consensus and maximum-likelihood ancestral state reconstruction (`IQ-TREE 3`, `GTR+G`) analysis of shared coding mutations and flanked-MSA exon splice borders across Palaeognathae topologies `T1`–`T3`, quantifying topology-dependent PTC incongruence (31.2–37.5% under `T1`, rising to 56.2–68.8% under `T2`/`T3`, consistent with empirical coverage-masked K80 neutral simulations) and demonstrating why multi-nucleotide frameshift indels and canonical splice-junction disruptions outperform isolated PTCs for deep-time pseudogene dating.

// =============================================================================
// 2. RESULTS
// =============================================================================
#heading(level: 1, numbering: none)[2. Results]

#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[2.1. Level 1 Annotation Audit and Forensic Dissection of Gnomon Coding Models]

Across the 18 sauropsid RefSeq reference assemblies audited via NCBI Datasets v2 (Table S1), the nine-gene canonical odontogenic panel (_ENAM_, _AMBN_, _AMTN_, _AMELX_, _MMP20_, _DSPP_, _ODAM_, _ODAPH_, _ACP4_) is completely represented in the mammalian outgroup _Homo sapiens_ `GRCh38.p14` (9/9 genes annotated as `PROTEIN_CODING`), whereas toothed sauropsids carry 4–7 officially annotated tooth genes per genome (7 in _Alligator mississippiensis_ [`Allmis`], 6 in _Crocodylus porosus_ [`Cropor`] and _Anolis carolinensis_ [`Anocar`], 5 in _Python bivittatus_ [`Pytbiv`], and 4 in _Gavialis gangeticus_ [`Gavgan`] and _Podarcis muralis_ [`Podmur`]). Missing official symbols in toothed species reflect nomenclature and pipeline gaps rather than biological absence: _Alligator_ _ODAPH_ is annotated as an uncharacterized locus (`LOC106740521`, `XP_019349100.2`, `NC_081825.1:164,672,404–164,676,187`), and functional _Anolis_ and _Podarcis_ _ODAPH_ orthologs are misannotated as non-coding RNAs (Table S1, Table S6).

Conversely, automated RefSeq annotation emitted `PROTEIN_CODING` models on enamel loci in 4 of the 12 edentulous genomes (Table S1): _Chrysemys picta_ (`Chrpic`, _ENAM_, chr6), _Chelonia mydas_ (`Chemyd`, _AMBN_, chr5), _Struthio camelus_ (`Strcam`, _AMELX_, chr1), and _Apteryx rowi_ (`Aptrow`, _AMELX_, unplaced scaffold `NW_020450766.1`). To test whether these represent genuine native inactivation rather than cross-species alignment divergence, we audited every annotated GenBank/Gnomon transcript (`XM_`), protein (`XP_`), genomic exon boundary, intron splice junction, flanking synteny, and reciprocal `BLASTp` hit directly on the native genome (Table S7):
- *All four loci are genuine orthologs flagged `LOW QUALITY PROTEIN` by NCBI Gnomon:* Whole-proteome `BLASTp` against 48,040 _Alligator_ proteins confirms orthology (`Chrpic` _ENAM_ `XP_065406651.1` → `XP_019346375.1`, 202 bits, _E_ = 3.4×10#super[−59]\; `Chemyd` _AMBN_ `XP_037759058.1` → `XP_019346385.1`, 252 bits, _E_ = 4.7×10#super[−80]\; `Strcam` _AMELX_ `XP_068787214.1` → `XP_014463267.1`, 93.6 bits under `-seg no`, _E_ = 1.3×10#super[−23]\; `Aptrow` _AMELX_ `XP_025935698.1` → `XP_014463267.1`, 121 bits, _E_ = 4.0×10#super[−34]), with both ratite _AMELX_ models retaining ancestral synteny adjacent to _ARHGAP6_ and zero assembly `N`-gaps within ±2 kb.
- *Three chromosome-level models are confirmed annotation-compensated pseudogenes:* In each case, NCBI Gnomon explicitly documented modifying the model relative to its native genome sequence to maintain an open reading frame. _C. picta_ _ENAM_ (`XM_065550579.1`, 253 aa partial model, 2 exons, contig N50 13.4 Mb) has a 761-bp native genomic CDS (non-multiple of 3) into which Gnomon inserted 1 synthetic `N` base at codon 33 (`NC_088796.1:129,121,140`, outside homopolymers; Level 3 `miniprot` further recovers 12 frameshifts and 4 PTCs across 713 aa). _C. mydas_ _AMBN_ (`XM_037903130.1`, 400 aa, 6 exons, contig N50 39.4 Mb) has a 1,201-bp native genomic CDS into which Gnomon inserted 2 synthetic `N` bases at codon 246 (`NC_051245.2:128,896,655`, inside a 4-C run) plus masked a native genomic `TAA` PTC at codon 44 (`128,902,406–128,902,408`) to `X` in `XP_037759058.1`, while skipping a disrupted `GT→AT` splice donor at Intron 8 detected by `miniprot`. _S. camelus_ _AMELX_ (`XM_068931113.1`, 190 aa, 3 exons, contig N50 28.8 Mb) has a 572-bp native genomic CDS patched by Gnomon with 1 non-homopolymer synthetic `N` insertion at codon 95 (`NC_090942.1:141,844,154`) and 3 non-homopolymer native PTCs (`TAG` codon 72, `TAA` codon 92, `TGA` codon 106), alongside a non-canonical Intron 1 (`CC..CC`, 849 bp).
- *One scaffold-level model carries an annotation-compensated lesion pending independent conspecific replication:* _A. rowi_ _AMELX_ (`XM_026079913.1`, 205 aa, 3 exons, scaffold-level `aptRow1`, contig N50 129.4 kb) has a 618-bp native genomic CDS with 0 annotated frameshifts, 1 native genomic `TAA` PTC at codon 177 (`NW_020450766.1:13,439–13,441`, adjacent to a 4-A run, masked to `X` in `XP_025935698.1`), and a non-canonical Intron 2 (`CA..CC`, 848 bp). Because no second conspecific _A. rowi_ assembly exists in NCBI GenBank/RefSeq, we classify `Aptrow` _AMELX_ conservatively as an annotation-compensated single-assembly lesion rather than a multi-assembly-confirmed pseudogene (Table S7).

In _Gallus gallus_ `GRCg7b` (`GCF_016699485.2`), zero of the nine odontogenic genes are annotated genome-wide in either NCBI RefSeq Annotation Release 106 (2021-12-23) or Ensembl Release 116 (`Gallus_gallus.bGalGal1.mat.broiler.GRCg7b.116.gtf.gz`, SHA-256 `407e3e27...`). All genomic coordinates below are reported as *1-based inclusive* intervals (`span = end − start + 1`; corresponding 0-based half-open BED intervals `[start − 1, end)` are provided in Table S2a). Between RefSeq _NRG1_ (`NC_052572.1:54,642,186–55,109,784`, `−`) and _LPL_ (`55,215,002–55,232,438`, `−`), the strictly intergenic interval (`NC_052572.1:55,109,785–55,215,001`, BED `55109784–55215001`) spans *105,217 bp* (endpoint difference `55,215,002 − 55,109,784 = 105,218 bp`; Table S2a). Within this gap, RefSeq Release 106 annotates a single uncharacterized lncRNA (`LOC112530610`, `55,180,611–55,194,886`, `+`) and Ensembl Release 116 annotates five intergenic lncRNAs (`ENSGALG00010014552` through `ENSGALG00010014761`), none of which overlap the enamel-gene relics recovered below (Figure 1B; @tab1).

#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[2.2. Level 2 Syntenic Recovery, Reciprocal Validation, and Coordinate Reconciliation]

As first noted for chicken _ENAM_ @alhashimi2010, the sauropsid _ENAM–AMBN–AMTN_ enamel cluster is conserved in strict synteny between _NRG1_ and _LPL_ (adjacent to _PSD3_) across all four extant reptile and bird clades (Figure 1A; Table S2a). Flanking _NRG1_ and _LPL_ anchors were recovered on the same chromosome or scaffold in 16 of 18 species (with single-anchor ±800-kb windows used for _Dromaius novaehollandiae_ [`Dronov`] and _Tinamus guttatus_ [`Tingut`] where one flanking symbol is unannotated in RefSeq), spanning 402.3–1,096.9 kb strictly intergenic in toothed Crocodylia and Squamata, 160.1–336.9 kb in Testudines, and 105.2–329.9 kb on chromosome Z (or homologous scaffolds) in Aves.

