#heading(level: 1, numbering: none)[3. Discussion]

#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[3.1. Calibrating Replication Against Original Contributions]

Our findings build directly upon the classic paleogenomic demonstration of independent tooth loss in birds and turtles @sire2008 @alhashimi2010 @meredith2013 @meredith2014. Specifically, prior work first identified the translocated _NRG1–LPL_ location of sauropsid _ENAM–AMBN–AMTN_, recovered avian _ENAM_/_AMBN_/_AMTN_ fragments in earlier draft assemblies, and documented physical loss of _ODAPH_ in edentulous lineages @alhashimi2010 @meredith2013 @meredith2014 @springer2016. Against that established baseline, our systematic 18-genome audit delivers four distinct empirical and methodological advances: (1) dual-database proof (`RefSeq 106` and `Ensembl 116`) that _all_ nine enamel/tooth loci remain completely unannotated in the modern chromosome-level chicken reference `GRCg7b`, accompanied by exact reconciled 1-based inclusive and 0-based BED coordinates with explicit evidence grading (whole-proteome reciprocal `BLASTx`-confirmed _ΨENAM_ Exon 8 and _ΨAMBN_ Exon 3, nucleotide- and synteny-confirmed _ΨENAM_ Exon 6, reciprocal-confirmed kakapo _ΨAMTN_, plus forensic native-CDS proof of four Gnomon `LOW QUALITY PROTEIN` coding false positives); (2) a 1,000-permutation + 20-aa block-shuffle 18-species benchmark showing that intact osteogenic IDPs (_IBSP_, _SPP1_) lose 25.5–51.5% of excess `tBLASTn` bitscore in birds—directly overlapping decayed enamel pseudogenes—while globular _LPL_ scores 112.6% of crocodylian levels; (3) demonstration that replacing truncated GenBank queries with full-length RefSeq orthologs recovers previously invisible pseudogene exons and splice disruptions (e.g., _Apteryx rowi_ _ENAM_) while requiring intron-length filters to reject distal low-complexity chaining (`protein SON` in _C. picta_); and (4) dual-aligner + IQ-TREE 3 ML ASR quantification showing that 31.2–37.5% of shared avian _ENAM_ Exon 8 PTCs are phylogenetically incongruent under the primary topology `T1` (rising to 56.2–68.8% under alternative Palaeognathae topologies `T2`/`T3`, Table S9)—matching empirical coverage-masked neutral stop-gain expectations (`16.0–19.0` expected shared PTCs vs. `16` observed, `40.0–44.4%` incongruent; Table S8)—and resolving the distinct roles of reference-vs.-ancestral codon shifts, CpG/`TGG` convergence, and reversible decay.

#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[3.2. Bidirectional Blind Spots of Automated Genome Annotation]

Automated annotation pipelines remain the entry point for most large-scale comparative genomic scans, yet our Level 1 and forensic native-CDS audits illustrate why annotation tables alone are unreliable for inferring gene presence or loss @weisman2022 @kirilenko2023. In _Gallus gallus_ `GRCg7b`, both NCBI RefSeq Release 106 and Ensembl Release 116 report zero enamel genes despite three unannotated, single-copy, syntenic exon relics residing in a normal-recombination region of chromosome Z (54th percentile of chrZ recombination bins) @elferink2010. Equally instructive is the converse error exposed by our native-CDS audit (Table S7): across four edentulous RefSeq assemblies (`Chrpic` _ENAM_, `Chemyd` _AMBN_, `Strcam` _AMELX_, `Aptrow` _AMELX_), NCBI Gnomon explicitly inserted synthetic `N` bases and/or masked native genomic stop codons to `X` to emit `LOW QUALITY PROTEIN` `PROTEIN_CODING` models over broken genomic loci (including non-canonical `CC..CC` and `CA..CC` introns in ratite _AMELX_), while functional toothed-reptile _ODAPH_ loci are labeled uncharacterized (`LOC106740521`) or non-coding RNA. Without synteny-anchored extraction and native genomic reading-frame verification, database-level presence/absence matrices systematically inject both false negatives and false positives into genotype–phenotype association frameworks @prudent2016 @kowalczyk2019 @saputra2021 @redlich2024.

#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[3.3. Why Intrinsically Disordered Proteins Break Naive Homology Metrics]

Enamel matrix proteins evolved within the SCPP family as flexible calcium- and mineral-binding scaffolds rich in Pro, Gln, Ser, and acidic residues @kawasaki2006 @kawasaki2021 @kawasaki2023 @chun2025. Standard `tBLASTn` scoring matrices and `SEG` low-complexity masking were engineered primarily for globular domains @wootton1993 @schaffer2001 @yu2003 @gertz2006. Our 1,000-permutation equidistant _Anolis_ benchmark (\~281 Ma to Crocodylia, Testudines, and Aves) isolates the resulting failure mode while explicitly separating divergence time from substitution rate: intact globular _LPL_ shows zero `SEG` sensitivity and *higher* protein identity and bitscore in birds (88.7% vs. 80.0% in Crocodylia; 112.6% of crocodylian excess bitscore), ruling out uniform avian rate acceleration across _NRG1–LPL_. Yet intact, functionally indispensable osteogenic IDPs (_IBSP_, _SPP1_) lose 25.5–51.5% of their excess bitscore in birds—dropping to `7.3–23.9 bits` for avian _SPP1_ (robust to 20-aa block-preserving shuffles), completely overlapping decayed avian _ENAM_ (`0.0–12.7 bits`) and _AMBN_ (`0.0–15.4 bits`). Our regression across 14 queries further confirms that null baselines depend simultaneously on query length ($rho = +0.651$ to $+0.702$) and `SEG`/compositional entropy (_r_ = −0.795 to −0.670), and that 20-permutation nulls are too noisy for heavily masked IDPs (`DSPP_Amis` CI width `25.87 → 0.70 bits` at `N = 1,000`).

This empirical overlap echoes theoretical predictions of homology detection failure in fast-evolving disordered regions @weisman2020 @chow2024 and yields a concrete methodological prescription for paleogenomics: *bitscore attenuation in IDPs should never be interpreted as pseudogenization without corroborating Level 3 coding lesions or Level 2 physical gap collapse.* Our empirical blind-spot calibration on chicken _ΨAMEL_ (`EU340348`) and our negative whole-proteome reciprocal `BLASTx` result on chicken _ΨENAM_ Exon 6 underscore the symmetric caveat: short or heavily truncated pseudogene exons fail protein reciprocity even when nucleotide `BLASTn` (_E_ = 5.3×10#super[−11] vs. _Alligator_) and syntenic collinearity firmly establish orthology.

#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[3.4. Homoplastic Premature Stops Versus Indel and Splice-Site Lesions]

Dating ancient phenotypic losses from shared inactivating mutations assumes that identical lesions across descendant clades reflect a single ancestral mutational event rather than convergence or reversal @meredith2013 @meredith2014 @emerling2026. Combining dual-aligner consensus with `IQ-TREE 3` ML ancestral state reconstruction, formal topology tests, and coverage-masked neutral stop-gain simulations demonstrates quantitatively why isolated PTCs carry a high ambiguity burden after >100 Myr of neutral evolution: on the primary topology `T1`, 37.5% (`6/16`, nominal linked-site 95% CI `15.2–64.6%`) of shared avian _ENAM_ Exon 8 PTCs are incongruent under irreversible parsimony, and 31.2% (`5/16`, `11.0–58.7%`) remain convergent even when `p.Ser1022Ter` is interpreted as a putative crown Neognathae stop with passerine reversal ($P_"stop" = 0.926$, `Anapla` uncovered), whereas alternative Palaeognathae topologies `T2` and `T3` increase parsimony incongruence to `62.5%` (`10/16`) and `68.8%` (`11/16`; `56.2%` and `62.5%` under reversible ML ASR; Table S9). Crucially, accounting for empirical per-species exon coverage resolves the apparent discrepancy between an unmasked 878-codon neutral simulation (`25.0–29.0` predicted shared PTCs, two-sided $p = 0.004"–"0.052$) and the `16` observed shared PTCs: when each simulated species is masked to its empirical dual-aligner codon coverage (`22.7–82.5%`, `0/878` codons covered across all 7 birds), baseline K80 evolution predicts a median of `16.0–19.0` shared PTCs (two-sided $p = 0.538"–"1.000$), `40.0–44.4%` unconditional incongruence (`37.5%` conditional median at $N_"shared" = 16$), and `0.0` crown-Neornithes PTC synapomorphies across $kappa in {2, 4, 6}$ and branch-length scales $0.65 times$ to $1.25 times$ (Table S8).

Equally important, explicit separation of reference coordinates from reconstructed ancestral states prevents three analytical overstatements: (i) *Alligator* reference residues (`XP_019346377.1`: `Pro311` `CCG`, `Phe601` `TTT`, `Trp1003` `TGG`) must not be conflated with reconstructed avian ancestral codons (Neornithes `CAA` [Gln] → Palaeognathae `CGA` [Arg] at codon 311; Neornithes `TAT` [Tyr] → Palaeognathae `TAA` at codon 601; ambiguous site-independent Neornithes ML `TGC` [Cys, $P = 0.343$] vs. `TGG` [Trp, $P = 0.168$] → Psittacopasserae `TGA` at codon 1003); (ii) distinct stop triplets differing by 1 transition (`TAA↔TAG` at codon 684; `TAA↔TGA` at codon 615) do not prove independent origins without phylogenetic and ancestral-state support; and (iii) absence of recovered exon sequence in a sister clade must not be conflated with confirmed absence of a mutation. Notably, #pcite(<meredith2014>) detected a shared crown-avian frameshift deletion in _ENAM_ across a much broader 48-bird sampling and inferred a single avian tooth loss \~116 Ma consistent with Mesozoic fossil transitions @brocklehurst2021; in our 8-bird RefSeq panel, neutral decay and partial exon loss erased crown-Neornithes PTC synapomorphies (matching our coverage-masked simulation median of `0`), whereas outgroup- and sister-verified lesions (Galloanserae _ENAM_ Exon 8 codon 619 frameshift and `p.Gly558Ter`; Palaeognathae _ENAM_ Exon 8 `p.Gln370Ter`, `p.Phe601Ter`, `p.Trp751Ter` and Exon 6 `AG→AC/GT`; Novaeratitae _ENAM_ Exon 6 `GT→AT`) and shared subclade lesions (Palaeognathae _AMBN_ Exon 4 16-nt deletion; `Chemyd` + `Chrpic` _AMBN_ Exon 4 1-nt insertion and `AG→AC` acceptor) provided minimum inactivation bounds for crown subclades.

#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[3.5. Limitations]

Six limitations delimit the scope of our conclusions and motivate targeted follow-up work:
1. *Phylogenetic sample size and non-independence:* Although our panel spans 18 sauropsid genomes across all four major clades, birds and turtles represent only _n_ = 2 independent evolutionary origins of edentulism. Because trait-association _p_-values across _n_ = 2 evolutionary replicates are severely underpowered and confounded by shared ancestry @saputra2021, all clade comparisons remain strictly descriptive and anchored by within-genome controls (_LPL_, _IBSP_, _SPP1_, crocodylian outgroups).
2. *Single-locus sampling, site linkage, and incomplete sister-clade coverage in Level 4:* The `16` shared avian _ENAM_ Exon 8 PTCs derive from a single linked exon across 7 aligned birds, so Clopper–Pearson 95% intervals (`15.2–64.6%` / `11.0–58.7%` on `T1`) reflect within-locus site sampling rather than independent loci. Furthermore, non-recovery of baseline _AMBN_ Exon 4 sequence in Neognathae (outside a diverged syntenic relic in `Anapla` carrying a 10-nt deletion at codon 109) and in `Gopevg`/`Pelsin` prevents definitive proof of clade exclusivity for the _AMBN_ Exon 4 indels beyond their sampled carrier sets (Table S5a).
3. *Assembly contiguity and single-assembly replication:* While 14 of 18 reference genomes are chromosome-level or complete T2T assemblies, four remain scaffold- or contig-level (`Gavgan` contig N50 14.8 kb; `Cropor` 34.1 kb; `Aptrow` `aptRow1` 129.4 kb; `Tingut` 3.85 Mb; Table S1). Notably, `Aptrow` _AMELX_ (`NW_020450766.1`) lacks a second conspecific assembly on NCBI, so its single compensated codon-177 `TAA` stop is classified cautiously (Table S7); by contrast, all key Level 4 synapomorphies replicate independently across high-contiguity assemblies (`Dronov` 24.2 Mb + `Strcam` 28.8 Mb + `Aptrow`; `Galgal` 18.8 Mb + T2T `Anapla` 80.2 Mb; `Chemyd` 39.4 Mb + `Chrpic` 13.4 Mb).
4. *Testudine basal sampling:* All four sampled turtles belong to Cryptodira (three Durocryptodira + one Trionychia, `Pelsin`). Inclusion of chromosome-level Pleurodira assemblies is required to test whether _ENAM_/_AMBN_ frameshifts predate crown Testudines around \~157–210 Ma @shaffer2013 @meredith2013.
5. *Assembly version alignment in auxiliary _ODAPH_ panel:* Primary _NRG1–LPL_ and _IBSP–SPP1_ analyses for _Pelodiscus sinensis_ use its current T2T reference genome (`GCF_052426305.1`, released 2025-09-03, annotated 2026-10-19), whereas auxiliary _ODAPH_ gap coordinates (`NC_134715.1`, 132-bp gap) originate from the prior RefSeq representative (`GCF_049634645.1`), explicitly noted in Table S6.
6. *Selection-intensity and transcriptional profiling:* Branch-specific $d_N\/d_S$ relaxation modeling (e.g., `HyPhy RELAX`) and embryonic oral RNA-seq verification of residual _ΨENAM_/_ΨAMBN_ transcription were outside the scope of this genomic audit.

